[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Caron, 2023-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6613171e0a6a2bbbd3e3635321380c7bf47437fc)
- venue: Friction
- citation count: 3
[method]
The paper used atomic force microscopy reciprocal sliding of a single SiOx-coated silicon tip on a Sylgard 184 PDMS surface to measure load- and velocity-dependent friction and stick-slip. A soft single-crystal silicon cantilever recorded topography, normal force, and lateral force maps over 10 by 10 µm areas while the tip was scanned back and forth at 20, 40, 80, and 160 µm/s under normal forces from -50 to 25 nN. Friction force maps were computed from forward and backward lateral force signals, and stick-slip events were extracted from line scans by identifying increasing lateral force sequences longer than five points and fitting them linearly to obtain lateral contact stiffness and sticking strength. The friction-force versus normal-force data were fitted with a linear model at tensile or low loads and with a JKR adhesive-contact shearing model at compressive loads; tip geometry was checked by scanning electron microscopy before and after testing, and post-test topography was imaged to assess wear. This method relates to the proposed PDMS mock-finger study because it probes the same soft elastomer, load and velocity dependence, adhesion, stick-slip, and surface-chemistry effects, but at a nanoscale single-asperity scale rather than macroscopic finger-scale sliding across six coatings.
[results]
The AFM friction data showed two regimes. For normal forces from -50 to -10 nN, friction increased linearly with normal force, and the linear slope decreased logarithmically as sliding velocity increased from 20 to 160 µm/s. For higher normal forces, friction followed the JKR F_n^(2/3) dependence, with fitted adhesion forces around 5 nN and shear strengths around 10 MPa, values that did not show a clear velocity trend. The scanning electron microscopy tip radius after testing was 27 nm, and corrected shear-strength estimates remained on the order of 10 MPa. Wear and surface degradation appeared only above about -15 nN, coinciding with the transition from linear to JKR-like friction; tested areas showed lower topography and increased friction, with the relative friction increase decaying logarithmically with increasing velocity. No clear abrasive plowing or debris was observed, supporting adhesive junction formation, shear, and rupture as the dominant mechanism.
[context]
Experiments were performed in ambient conditions at 293 K and 50% relative humidity on a PDMS sample prepared from Sylgard 184 mixed 10:1 and cured for 15 min at 90 °C. The as-prepared PDMS had an AFM roughness Rq of about 1 nm. The AFM used a soft single-crystal silicon cantilever with bending stiffness 0.176 N/m and torsion stiffness 97.786 N/m, calibrated by force-distance curves and thermal noise analysis. Sliding was reciprocal over 10 by 10 µm areas at velocities of 20, 40, 80, and 160 µm/s and normal forces from -50 to 25 nN. The SiOx tip radius was 27 nm after testing. These conditions are relevant to the proposed research plan because they demonstrate that PDMS friction is strongly governed by adhesion, elastic junction elongation, stick-slip, and velocity-dependent relaxation, but the paper does not test the proposed macroscopic mock finger, six surface coatings, 5-45 mm/s velocities, or gram-scale normal loads.
[end related work 1]



[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Tomlinson et al., 2011-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c8bfb3872e88c45c1acc1528be4c1d2df59315d9)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 58
[method]
The paper used a conventional friction measurement and modelling approach for finger contact on ridged surfaces. A human finger was slid across fine-ridged surfaces made of brass, steel, and aluminium. Each material had five ridge geometries, and each geometry was tested at five normal loads, giving 75 friction-force measurements. The measured friction forces were compared with model predictions for a finger contacting triangular ridges, using a fixed loss fraction of 0.45 and equations combining adhesion, interlocking, and hysteresis contributions. The model outputs were evaluated by linear regression of predicted versus measured friction force, and the friction mechanism contributions were decomposed for brass. In the surface-design analysis, coefficients of friction were compared across ridge widths and heights. This is related to the proposed research method because it uses conventional friction-force and friction-coefficient measurements together with surface geometry descriptors, similar to the traditional metrics the proposed method plans to compare against instability-based predictions.
[results]
The best predicted-versus-measured fit was reported for brass, corresponding to a slope of 0.96 and R-squared of 0.78. The other fits were less accurate: one had slope 0.54 and R-squared 0.76, and the other had slope 0.66 and R-squared 0.54. The text identifies steel as having the lowest precision, consistent with the lowest R-squared value, and attributes the overprediction for steel and aluminium to blunt ridge tips and inconsistent steel ridge profiles. Mechanism decomposition for brass showed that adhesive friction dominated for small ridges, interlocking friction became large for larger ridges and higher loads, and hysteresis friction contributed little. Coefficients of friction changed little across most textures until ridge width reached 0.28 mm and height reached 108 microns, where friction increased, and increased substantially for the largest texture with 0.3 mm width and 250 microns height.
[context]
Testing used one female participant aged 25 years, with the middle finger of the dominant hand sliding perpendicular to the ridge pattern. Ridge heights were 4.75, 33.5, 42.5, 105, and 250 microns, and ridge widths were 0.1, 0.17, 0.23, 0.28, and 0.3 mm. The model assumed triangular ridges, a single loss fraction, and applied flat-surface adhesion and particle interlocking equations to ridged skin contact. The study did not measure contact angle hysteresis, classify frictional instabilities, or test human discrimination performance. Therefore, it is relevant to the proposed research plan as a conventional friction-coefficient and surface-geometry baseline, but it does not directly test instability-based tactile discrimination.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 2]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Zhang et al., 2017-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c2abc15c408b0b8ef82c77555f23af75aa64115e)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 24
[method]
The paper used a combined human perception and in vivo tactile friction measurement approach on five stainless steel EN-1.4301 tiles, including four textured samples and one reference surface. Participants were blindfolded and asked to slide the middle finger of the non-dominant hand toward the body over a 30 mm stroke, then rate perceived roughness, stickiness, and comfort on a 0 to 10 scale. Kinetic friction was measured with a multi-axis force/torque transducer that recorded normal and tangential forces at 100 Hz, and the coefficient of friction was calculated as the ratio of friction force to normal load. Surface roughness was characterized using a 3D areal confocal microscope over 1 mm by 1 mm areas with a 20x objective, 63 nm height resolution, and a 4 um filter, reporting the 3D roughness parameter Sa. This method relates to the proposed research method because it uses the same conventional descriptors named in the plan, average friction coefficient and surface roughness, as baseline metrics for comparing against instability-based predictions, although it does not measure contact angle hysteresis or classify frictional instability phases.
[results]
The friction measurements showed substantial participant-to-participant variability in friction force and coefficient of friction on the same surface. The average coefficient of friction was highest for the crater texture labeled 1HV90. Across samples, higher 3D surface roughness Sa was associated with lower tactile friction, with a coefficient of determination of 0.8313. The reference stainless steel sample had the lowest measured Sa. The average normal load was 0.25 ± 0.12 N, ranging from 0.13 N to 0.63 N, and lower normal loads were slightly associated with higher coefficient of friction. Sliding velocity ranged from 25 mm/s to 124 mm/s, and skin hydration ranged from 62 AU to 120 AU with an average of 90.9 ± 17.8 AU. The provided excerpt reports these friction and roughness outcomes rather than quantitative perception-rating results. These findings show that conventional average friction coefficient and roughness can capture some surface-related differences, but they are also affected by human motion, load, hydration, and contact area, which supports the research plan’s motivation for comparing instability-based metrics against these traditional descriptors.
[context]
Sixteen subjects, eight female and eight male, aged 20 to 30 years, participated in a controlled laboratory at 20 ± 1 °C and 50% ± 10% relative humidity. Fingertips were cleaned with alcohol and air-dried for 10 minutes before testing, and skin hydration was measured with a corneometer before the experiments. Perception tests used blindfolds, random sample order, unlimited exploration time, and 0 to 10 ratings for perceived roughness, stickiness, and comfort. Friction tests used five repetitions per sample, no external load, light-touch normal forces below 1 N, a 30 mm stroke, and velocity recorded by the force transducer. The force/torque transducer had a resolution of 25 mN in the normal direction and 12.5 mN in the tangential direction. The test contact areas were approximately 30 mm by 30 mm. This context is relevant to the research plan because the paper’s human friction data were collected under natural, uncontrolled sliding velocities and pressures, unlike the proposed mock-finger phase maps and controlled human forced-choice trials, and therefore illustrate the variability that instability-based metrics are intended to reduce.
[end related work 2]

[start related work 3]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Sergachev et al., 2021-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/322f92233f72b50913c369245230611ac0f61cfd)
- venue: Friction
- citation count: 3
[method]
The paper used an in-vivo reciprocating friction measurement on a human index finger pad to generate conventional friction descriptors. A silicone rubber sample with square-packed ellipsoidal asperities was mounted on a linear reciprocating tribometer and slid over the subject's index finger, which rested on a 30-degree wedge, while normal loads were applied with static weights. Sliding was performed parallel and perpendicular to the major asperity radii at 2 mm/s over a 20 mm stroke, with three consecutive strokes per load after three preparatory strokes at 0.2 N. Forces were recorded with a 6-axis transducer at 100 Hz, and a MATLAB routine extracted the stable sliding region, removed a 0.5 Hz sinusoidal noise component from the drive mechanism, and computed average dynamic friction coefficients. Surface topography was measured with a 3D optical profiler, and reference roughness was characterized by average Sa. This method is related to the proposed research method because it produces the traditional metrics that the proposed plan uses as baselines, especially average friction coefficient and surface roughness, although it does not measure contact angle hysteresis or classify frictional instabilities.
[results]
Non-textured reference surfaces showed adhesion-like power-law behavior, with friction coefficients decreasing from about 6.5 at low normal load to about 2.5 at high normal load. One reference surface with Sa around 150 nm had lower friction than references with Sa between 40 and 60 nm, indicating that roughness affected the conventional friction metric. For 30 μm asperity height, parallel sliding gave the lowest friction, with peak coefficients of 2.7 for E100 and 2.3 for E150 at 1 N, followed by a decline toward reference values as full contact developed; perpendicular sliding produced slightly higher friction, with the largest directional difference below 0.5 N. For 50 μm asperity height, parallel peak coefficients were lower, 2.2 for E100 and 2.0 for E150, and the height effect was statistically significant with paired t-test p-values of 1.2e-5 for E100 and 8.6e-6 for E150. The directional difference was strongest at low loads and diminished as the contact transitioned to full contact.
[context]
The study used one human subject, four measurement series over two days, duplicate textured samples, and a non-textured reference in each set. Normal loads were 0.2 to 2 N in 0.2 N steps and then up to 4 N in 0.5 N steps; sample order was randomized, but loads were increased sequentially because finger pad recovery was slow. The textured samples were 10 mm thick silicone rubber with an approximate elastic modulus of 0.2 MPa, 200 μm lateral spacing, 25 μm minor asperity radius, major radii of 50 or 75 μm, and asperity heights of 30 or 50 μm. Ambient conditions were 22±1 °C and 43±7% relative humidity; hands were washed and air-dried for 15 min, and skin hydration remained stable at 50±4 arbitrary units. Samples were cleaned with acetone and alcohol, rinsed, and stored in vacuum for 12 h. The transducer had 6 mN normal and 2 mN tangential resolution. The method does not provide instability phase maps, contact angle hysteresis, or human perceptual accuracy, so its relevance is as a conventional friction and roughness baseline rather than a direct test of the proposed instability-based prediction approach.
[end related work 3]

[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Chaudhury et al., 2014-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/690276dcd975b048dbab78fc363f584466da7b2a)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 27
[method]
The paper fabricated a chemically patterned silicon wafer by vapor deposition of decyltrichlorosilane. A 0.29 mm thick 2-inch silicon wafer was flame cleaned, placed in a polystyrene petri dish, and covered with a 0.12 mm transparency-sheet template cut into alternating 3 mm and 9 mm stripes. A filter paper soaked with decyltrichlorosilane was placed under the lid, and the wafer was exposed to silane vapor for 1 minute. The exposed regions became hydrophobic while masked regions remained hydrophilic, producing a 25% hydrophilic and 75% hydrophobic striped surface. The wafer was then mounted on a copper cylinder in a steam condensation heat exchanger, with steam above the wafer and cold water cooling the opposite copper face; thermocouples measured axial temperature gradients and a camera recorded condensation. This method is related to the proposed research plan because it uses silane vapor deposition on silicon wafers to create surface-chemistry contrasts without adding a separate bulk material, similar to the proposed minimal tactile surfaces. However, the paper uses the pattern for condensation and drop transport, not for measuring frictional instabilities or human tactile discrimination.
[results]
Water droplets spread on the hydrophilic stripes and were confined at stripe boundaries, while droplets on the hydrophobic stripes had an average contact angle of about 93 degrees, with similar angles across four drops indicating no significant wettability gradient within a hydrophobic stripe. Under steam flow with the backside cooled by 19 C water, condensation occurred as a thin film on hydrophilic stripes and as discrete drops on hydrophobic stripes. Drops moved laterally from hydrophobic to hydrophilic stripes, with a typical average velocity of about 1 cm/s, and were removed by merging with the hydrophilic film. Heat flux estimated from the copper temperature gradient and from collected condensate mass gave similar values, although the paper does not report a single numerical heat-flux value. A vertical mist experiment on the same type of striped surface also produced directed motion from hydrophobic to hydrophilic zones, supporting coalescence with an absorbing boundary as a main mechanism. The paper provides no friction force, instability frequency, or human perception measurements, so its quantitative outcomes are limited to wettability, condensation morphology, drop motion, and heat-flux consistency.
[context]
Key conditions included a 0.29 mm thick 2-inch silicon wafer, a 0.12 mm transparency template, 3 mm hydrophilic and 9 mm hydrophobic stripe widths, one minute of decyltrichlorosilane vapor exposure in a closed petri dish, steam generation at 3.6 kg/hr, convective cooling with 19 C water, four thermocouples inserted through laser-drilled holes in the copper cylinder, and video recording with a regular camera. The surface was characterized with 1 microliter water droplets. The heat exchanger used a copper cylinder in a Teflon jacket, with the wafer clipped to the upper surface and steam contacting the wafer. These conditions are relevant to the proposed tactile-surface work as a reproducible silane-vapor-deposition protocol for creating chemically distinct silicon regions, but they are thermal condensation conditions rather than controlled finger pressure and velocity conditions needed to map frictional instabilities.
[end related work 1]


[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Skedung et al., 2018-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/475c09ef715548a6b38bdb18d1fd4d6811c6dbe1)
- venue: Tribology letter
- citation count: 17
[method]
The paper measured human tactile friction on ten smooth glass coatings, prepared as 1500 total samples with 150 samples per coating, using a ForceBoard equipped with horizontal and tangential load cells. A single female experimenter stroked each surface with her dominant index finger at approximately 20 mm/s, an applied load of 1.2 ± 0.15 N, a stroking distance of about 80 mm, and a finger sliding angle of approximately 30°. Force data were sampled at 100 Hz, turning points were removed, and friction coefficients were calculated as the ratio of tangential friction force to vertical applied load, with absolute values reported. Each sample received six friction measurements, each from a fresh track, and the ten surfaces were tested in six randomized series. The paper also characterized the surfaces by water contact angle, contact angle hysteresis, surface free energy, and atomic force microscopy roughness from 2 × 2 µm images. These methods provide a baseline friction-coefficient and surface-chemistry characterization, but the paper did not classify friction traces into steady sliding, slow frictional waves, or stiction spikes. It only noted stick-slip behavior and stated that the 100 Hz sampling rate was too low to reliably extract oscillation frequencies. This contrasts with the proposed method, which would use higher-rate 550 Hz traces and a mock finger or human exploration to define instability phases and map them across velocity and load.
[results]
Perceptual dimension 1 showed a statistically significant relationship with water contact angle, although the paper did not report the correlation coefficient or p-value. Correlation of dimension 1 with friction coefficient was not statistically significant overall. When the surfaces were split into high-hydrophilicity/low-hydrophobicity samples #1-5 and low-hydrophilicity/high-hydrophobicity samples #6-10, the analysis suggested that water contact angle remained the main explanatory parameter for #6-10, while friction coefficient was more important for #1-5. Sample #6 was an exception, showing high friction, high contact angle hysteresis, and frictional hysteresis. The authors suggested that a finger friction model is more meaningful for water contact angles below about 100° and less meaningful above 100°, but noted that only one high-friction, high-water-contact-angle sample was available, so this interpretation should be treated cautiously. Dimension 2 showed no systematic relationship with the measured physical parameters. Samples displaying the most stick-slip behavior were spread across dimension 2, and the 100 Hz sampling rate was too low to reliably extract frequencies, although the observed frequencies appeared high enough to be detectable by Pacinian mechanoreceptors.
[context]
The experiments used float glass substrates coated with homogeneous thin films under 10 nm thick, with water contact angles ranging from 0° to approximately 120° and similarly low roughness. Friction measurements were performed in a climate-controlled room at 22 °C and 45% relative humidity. Skin hydration was monitored before each series and remained in the range of 65-70 arbitrary units, described as a moist finger. Water contact angle was measured at six droplet locations/sides with a goniometer and re-evaluated at the time of friction measurement; no significant change in water contact angle over the experiment was observed. Surface free energy was derived from contact angles measured with water, ethylene glycol, and diiodomethane. Roughness was measured with an atomic force microscope using 2 × 2 µm images, an aluminum-coated silicon tip, a resonance frequency of about 190 kHz, and a force constant of 48 N/m. The single-operator, fixed-speed, fixed-load design and 100 Hz acquisition limit the ability to quantify instability dynamics, motivating the proposed multi-velocity and multi-load instability-categorization approach and comparison against average friction coefficient, roughness, and contact angle hysteresis.
[end related work 1]

