- in_text_citation: [(Li et al., 2024-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ad53062ed60bef45cadbaea85bd65a643d40cbab)
- venue: Friction
- citation count: 6
[method]
The paper used controlled human finger-surface friction tests to characterize load- and velocity-dependent friction of finger skin on smooth and textured surfaces. A participant’s index finger was pressed and slid against test surfaces while a universal friction tester recorded friction force and normal load; indentation tests with a 12.6 mm steel ball and high-speed imaging of the apparent contact area were used to estimate the finger’s elastic response. The friction data were fitted with a two-term model separating an adhesion term from a deformation-resistance term, which is conceptually related to the proposed method’s focus on competition between adhesion and finger elasticity, although the paper did not categorize time-resolved friction traces into steady sliding, slow frictional waves, or stiction spikes.
[results]
The friction coefficient decreased with increasing normal load and showed substantial overlap across textured surfaces. At small normal loads, the smooth surface produced approximately twice the friction force of the textured surfaces. The fitted adhesion parameter was 3.6, the deformation-resistance parameter was 0.04, and the elasticity-related parameter was 0.52. At 5 N, adhesion contributed about 83% of total friction and deformation resistance about 17%; the load at which deformation resistance would equal adhesion was estimated at 25 N. Changing sliding velocity from 2 to 50 mm/s produced only a slight decrease in friction, and the authors noted that higher-velocity skin friction can involve unsteady phenomena such as stick-slip, but they did not quantify distinct instability phases.
[context]
The tests involved one 26-year-old male participant with sanitized and dried fingers. Indentation was performed from 0 to 0.7 N at 0.1 mm/s using a 12.6 mm steel ball and a 2D force sensor with a 0-10 N range and 0.5 mN resolution; contact area was imaged at 500 fps with a 1280 by 1024 pixel camera and a 10 N 3-axis force sensor. Friction testing used smooth and textured surfaces, with textured surfaces treated as having a surface ratio of 0.5, and sliding velocities of 2 and 50 mm/s under constant-velocity conditions. These conditions provide a human-finger baseline for adhesion-dominated friction and possible stick-slip, but they do not establish the proposed instability phase map across pressure and velocity.
[end related work 3]

