[method]
The paper describes a slider-track friction apparatus for elastomer friction testing: an elastomer sample (7 x 7 x 0.28 cm^3) was glued to an aluminum plate (10 x 10 x 1 cm^3), and the slider surface was hand-lapped below Tg with 23 µm SiC abrasive to a reproducible rms roughness of 1.3 µm. The opposing track was a rough glass block (15 x 15 x 1 cm^3) prepared in the same way, chosen to avoid polymer interdiffusion and contact-age effects. Dynamic mechanical properties (storage and loss moduli) were measured from 0.1 to 750 Hz and WLF-transformed, with sample #1 used for quantitative presentation. This setup is related to the proposed method and research plan because it is a mechanical friction measurement system for an elastomer slider on a controlled surface, analogous to the plan's mock-finger friction trace collection; however, the excerpt does not specify a 550 Hz sampling rate or nine repeated traces per mass-velocity combination.
[results]
No friction force trace outcomes, instability event counts, or statistical comparisons are reported in the provided excerpt. The only reported outcome is that the WLF transform yielded shift factors and that most results were qualitatively independent of sample, with quantitative results presented for sample #1.
