- in_text_citation: [(Viswanathan, and Sundaram, 2017-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/cb1e347ec77f475a86c8b034b73f577ea814ccaf)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 41
[method]
The paper used a controlled adhesive sliding experiment in which a polydimethylsiloxane slab was pressed against a cylindrical plano-convex lens and the slab was translated at constant remote velocity while the friction force was recorded with a piezoelectric dynamometer. Normal load was varied by changing the contact width 2a under constant displacement, and the interface was simultaneously imaged with a high-speed microscope to identify the physical source of force oscillations. This method is related to the proposed method because both collect friction force traces across controlled velocity and normal-load conditions to detect stick-slip or frictional instability events; however, the paper used simultaneous in situ imaging to resolve wave mechanisms and did not specify a 550 Hz force sampling rate or nine replicate traces per condition.
[results]
Stick-slip was the dominant sliding mode for remote velocities from 0.05 to 10 mm/s, and three distinct force-trace modes were linked to three interface waves: Schallamach waves, slip pulses, and separation pulses. The number of waves per second equaled the force oscillation frequency, and the product of unit slip per wave and wave frequency matched the remote velocity within a few percent. For separation pulses, unit slip and frequency both depended on velocity and 2a, with the product obeying the velocity relation within 6%; for Schallamach waves, unit slip was constant and frequency was approximately proportional to velocity, with a nonzero intercept and nonlinear trend; for slip pulses, the velocity relation held to within less than 2% over several runs.
[context]
The PDMS was Sylgard 184 mixed 10:1, cured for 6 hours at 100 C and 18 hours at room temperature, with slab thickness between 20 and 30 mm and sliding length of 22 mm or 90 mm for Schallamach-wave tests. The lens had a 16.25 mm cylindrical radius and formed a 25 mm long adhesive contact of variable width 2a. Experiments used a 60 second wait after contact, constant-displacement normal loading, remote velocities from 10 µm/s to 20 mm/s, sliding distances of at least 30 mm, and force measurement with a Kistler 9254 dynamometer. Imaging used backlighting, a Nikon Optiphot microscope, and a PCO dimax CMOS camera at 100 to 5000 fps with 2.8 µm per pixel. These conditions are analogous to the proposed mock-finger force-trace testing in that they define a soft elastic adhesive contact and vary velocity and normal load, but the paper’s load parameter was contact width 2a rather than applied mass, and its diagnostic power came from simultaneous high-speed interface imaging rather than force traces alone.
[end related work 2]

