- in_text_citation: [(Gao, and Wang, 2022-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2c49d57dd06c0fbe068552e9a37e9602566e9107)
- venue: Tribology letter
- citation count: 6
[method]
The paper used a dynamic sliding-friction experiment on mold-injected silicone specimens with smooth and toothed contact planes, placed on a metal platform with Ra0.4 roughness. A load block applied normal load, and a rope connected the left end of the silicone to a force sensor while the platform was driven right at 100 mm/min; the sensor and data logger recorded friction force at 25 Hz, and the sliding friction coefficient was interpreted using a dynamic friction measurement approach combined with the classical friction law. In parallel, DIC optical non-contact measurement was used: cameras captured randomly distributed surface speckles during sliding, and digital image correlation algorithms compared speckle patterns to compute surface deformation and strain. This method relates to the proposed research method because both collect repeated friction force traces under controlled normal loads and velocities to observe instability events in soft elastomeric materials, and both support the broader plan to map frictional instabilities in a soft mock-finger system; however, the paper uses a simpler silicone-on-metal setup, a single velocity, five replicates per load, and lower 25 Hz force sampling than the proposed 550 Hz, nine-trace mass-velocity design.
[results]
The method revealed a multi-phase sliding response consisting of preliminary adjustment, deformation, stick-slip, and steady-state motion, with strain distribution described as chaotic, symmetric, and gradual. Stick-slip became more pronounced with increasing normal load within the tested range, indicated by wider macroscopic deformation, larger separation force, and more frequent events. For a fixed normal load, jump amplitude decreased over time, attributed to contact time and post-jump recontact normal force affecting adhesion. Deformation waves appeared mainly during transitions from preliminary adjustment to deformation and after stick-slip; the first wave speed was similar across loads, while waves during stick-slip transmitted faster as load increased. The provided sections do not report numerical friction coefficients, statistical significance, or baseline comparisons; the quantitative evidence consists of the tested load levels, replication count, sampling rate, and observed qualitative trends.
[context]
Silicone was fabricated by 3D-printed mold injection, mixing substrate and curing agent 1:1, degassing, and curing at 25 C for 10 h. Tests used additional normal loads of 0, 0.01, 0.02, and 0.03 N excluding specimen weight, with no additional load for the toothed surface; five independent tests were run per load. Friction force was sampled at 25 Hz, and high-speed cameras recorded surface speckles at approximately 70 fps for DIC. The metal platform had Ra0.4 roughness, and the relative sliding speed was 100 mm/min. DIC was used because contact deformation and strain measurements were considered susceptible to uncontrollable environmental interference. These conditions are lower in force sampling rate, replication, and load/velocity range than the proposed method, which uses 550 Hz, nine traces per mass-velocity combination, and multiple velocities/masses to build instability maps for a PDMS mock finger across chemically varied surfaces.
[end related work 3]

