[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Juvekar, and Singh, 2024-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d7d7c26b9b7c7929fb09e2ca7c96e59fe4dd8ddd)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 0
[method]
The paper validates a stick-slip friction model by analyzing published friction traces from a gelatine gel block sliding on glass. It uses steady-sliding shear-stress-versus-velocity data from 140 to 2000 µm/s and stick-slip stress-time traces at pulling velocities of 50, 100, and 350 µm/s. The steady-sliding fit is performed in two steps: first, in the 800-2000 µm/s range, velocity-dependent bond aging is neglected and four parameters are estimated with u0 fixed to 1; second, in the 140-800 µm/s range, the aging term is included to estimate two additional parameters. For stick-slip, the paper extracts cycle-by-cycle features from the stress-time plots, including critical stress, stick-phase slope, slip stress, stick interval, and slip velocity, and compares them with model predictions and with the block stiffness. This is related to the proposed method because both rely on repeated friction traces across controlled velocities to identify instability cycles and extract cycle-level metrics, but the paper uses existing gel-glass data and model fitting rather than 550 Hz mock-finger acquisition or nine replicates per mass-velocity condition.
[results]
The two-step steady-sliding fit produced N0 = 1.22e14 m^-2, tau = 224 µs, lambdaM/kT = 2.47e12 m^-2, delta l_m = 1.074 µm, tau_a = 899 µs, and V_a = 360 µm/s. The model predicted a critical velocity of 125 µm/s and a critical stress of 259 Pa. In the stick-slip cycles, critical stress was 255-264 Pa, reported as 259 ± 5 Pa, and was nearly independent of pulling velocity. Stick-phase slopes were 16.3-116 Pa/s, and the corresponding G/h values were 424-457 kPa/m, close to the nominal 400 kPa/m. Slip stress was 542-800 Pa, with the first 100 µm/s cycle at 658 Pa higher than most other cycles. Stick intervals were 7.51-18.3 s, and slip velocities were 702-1026 µm/s. The model curves were described as good fits to both steady-sliding and stick-slip data.
[context]
The system was a 5 wt% gelatine gel with bloom strength 300 at 20 C, sliding on glass. The gel shear modulus was 4 kPa and block height was 10 mm, giving a nominal stiffness of about 400 kPa/m. The block had a trapezoidal contact geometry with B = 50 mm and l = 30 mm, producing a geometric correction of 0.766. The data were extracted from published figures rather than newly acquired at a stated sampling rate, and the analysis focused on multiple stick-slip cycles at three pulling velocities rather than nine traces per mass-velocity combination. This context matters for the proposed research plan because the paper demonstrates that cycle-level friction-trace features can characterize stick-slip instability, but it does not establish the high-rate trace collection, replicate count, or mass/velocity matrix used in the proposed mock-finger experiments.
[end related work 4]

