- in_text_citation: [(Bochereau et al., 2017-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0710910fe3b849426f987ec89e16bc83b030ba73)
- venue: IEEE Transactions on Haptics
- citation count: 33
[method]
The paper used a tribometer with high-speed total internal reflection imaging to record fluctuating friction forces and real contact area during dynamic finger-glass contact. A participant pressed an index finger on a glass plate at about 1 N while the plate was vibrated at 10, 20, 50, 100, 200, 500, and 1000 Hz with a few millimetres amplitude, and absence of slip was visually confirmed. Tangential traction force and real contact area were averaged over 2.0 s and three trials per condition. This method is related to the proposed research plan's mechanical testing stage because it quantifies dynamic friction and contact variables that could be used to characterize instability differences, although the paper did not select six surface pairs, use silane-coated surfaces, or classify friction traces into steady sliding, slow frictional waves, and stiction spikes.
[results]
Interfacial shear stress, defined as tangential force divided by real contact area increment, ranged from 0 to 1.2 MPa. The real contact area increment over 2 s was relatively insensitive to loading rate, whereas interfacial shear stress tended to decrease with loading rate. Moist fingers were less sensitive to loading rate and showed more consistently lower values below 0.4 MPa than dry fingers. The measured normal loads were 0.94 plus or minus 0.60 N for dry conditions and 0.75 plus or minus 0.30 N for moist conditions. The paper contrasted these values with about 5 kPa reported for fully hydrated inner forearm stratum corneum, noting the finger ridges in the experiment were only briefly exposed to sweat and were less compliant.
[context]
One 27-year-old participant used her index finger; the skin was cleaned and then dabbed with a dry or moist tissue before measurements to create dry and moist states. The apparatus included a rigid tribometer with load cells, an electrodynamic voice-coil motor, a Foucault-current damper, and a high-speed camera with a zoom lens and 0.75x attachment, achieving pixel sizes down to 10 nm. Prism-based frustrated total internal reflection imaged junction-level real contact area, while direct illumination total internal reflection imaged gross and ridge contact area. The dynamic tests used a single glass plate, 2.0 s trials, three trials per condition, vibration frequencies of 10 to 1000 Hz, amplitudes of a few millimetres, and normal loads near 1 N. The real contact area value used for traction was based on the difference between final and initial contact area to reduce error from initial moisture variation.
[end related work 1]

Processing method: Context: To choose human test stimuli that span meaningful instability differences, Method: We will select six pairs of surfaces with varying ranges of instability differences based on mechanical testing results.
Processing method: Context: To define the instability categories used in mechanical and human comparisons, Method: We will categorize friction traces into three broad phases based on competition between adhesion and finger elasticity: steady sliding (constant friction force with high-frequency oscillations), slow frictional waves (large oscillations at frequencies lower than microscopic stick-slip), and stiction spikes (singular large-magnitude events preceding smoother traces).
Processing method: Context: To provide controlled stimuli that differ only in surface chemistry, Method: We will construct "minimal" tactile surfaces using vapor deposition of silanes onto silicon wafers.

