- in_text_citation: [(Zhou et al., 2023-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0fc0ee667ac306ac827c67058c1d3a58daa65558)
- venue: Chinese Journal of Mechanical Engineering
- citation count: 5
[method]
The paper used human-finger sliding protocols that are relevant to the proposed constrained human exploration. In the active protocol, a participant actively slid the index finger in the x-direction over PTFE specimens while applying a normal load of about 1 N, and a six-axis force/torque transducer recorded normal and tangential forces. In the passive finger-down protocol, a participant's index finger was fixed in supports and tape, a PTFE specimen on a motorized table was loaded against the finger, and the table imposed a controlled tangential sliding speed; a three-axis load cell and contact microphone recorded force and vibration. The passive finger-down protocol is the closest analogue to the proposed method because it constrains the human finger to a defined contact configuration, load, and speed, similar to the plan's goal of matching mechanical testing conditions, but it is device-driven rather than participant-driven vertical downward motion. The active protocol is relevant as a contrast because it allowed participant-controlled velocity and showed the variability that the proposed vertical downward constraint is intended to reduce.
[results]
Active sliding gave a mean friction coefficient of 0.67 for one measured PTFE sample, with the smooth PTFE#1 exceeding 1 and the rougher PTFE#2 and PTFE#3 lower and similar. The participant-controlled sliding speed averaged 10.06 ± 3.07 mm/s over about 60 mm, indicating significant velocity oscillation, and the FFT of the PTFE#3 friction signal showed dominant frequencies of 96.21 Hz and 234.42 Hz with relatively low magnitude. Passive finger-down sliding produced mean friction coefficients of 0.95, 0.88, and 0.64 for PTFE#1, PTFE#2, and PTFE#3, respectively, from the steady sliding region. The normal force remained near 1 N without significant fluctuation, and the friction and vibration signals showed continuous movement without obvious oscillations. The FFT for PTFE#3 showed dominant frequencies of 95.19 Hz and 508.08 Hz, and an additional data set showed a similar trend, supporting repeatability.
[context]
The active tests used three PTFE specimens with increasing surface roughness, a 10 kHz six-axis force/torque transducer with 65 N tangential and 200 N normal measurement ranges and 12.5 mN tangential and 25 mN normal resolutions, and a target normal load near 1 N. The passive finger-down tests used one male participant, the same three PTFE specimens, three trials per specimen, an imposed normal load of 1 ± 0.1 N, an orientation angle of 30 ± 1°, and a sliding speed of 10 mm/s. The passive setup used a 2 kHz data acquisition system, a three-axis load cell with 100 N range and 5 mN resolution, a contact microphone with 40 V/mm sensitivity, MCU-based control, a servo motor for table height, a vibration-reduction platform, and an emergency stop. Hand dominance was not specified. Neither protocol used the proposed participant-driven vertical downward motion, a PDMS mock finger, or the plan's three-alternative forced choice surface discrimination task.
[end related work 1]

Processing method: Context: To constrain human exploration to the same motion regime as the mock finger, Method: Participants will use only vertical downward motions with their dominant index fingers to mimic mechanical testing conditions.
Processing method: Context: To test whether instability-based predictions outperform conventional surface descriptors, Method: We will compare these correlations against traditional metrics including average friction coefficient, surface roughness, and contact angle hysteresis.

