    "contradictions": "The strongest contradiction to the claim that instability-based predictions will outperform conventional descriptors comes from [(Zhang et al., 2017-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c2abc15c408b0b8ef82c77555f23af75aa64115e), because it reports that higher 3D surface roughness Sa, measured with a 3D confocal microscope over 1 mm by 1 mm areas, was associated with lower tactile friction with a coefficient of determination of 0.8313, and that the average coefficient of friction was highest for the crater texture labeled 1HV90. As newer work, this partially contradicts the more deterministic geometry-friction modelling in [(Tomlinson et al., 2011-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c8bfb3872e88c45c1acc1528be4c1d2df59315d9) by showing that the same surface can yield variable friction across participants and that roughness alone can explain much of the friction variation. This suggests that conventional roughness and average friction metrics can already capture meaningful surface-related differences, which would weaken the proposed method if instability frequencies do not explain additional variance in human accuracy or response time. The contradiction is tempered by the small surface set of five stainless steel tiles, the absence of quantitative perception-rating outcomes in the provided excerpt, and the fact that the study did not measure instability phases or contact angle hysteresis. The next strongest contradiction is from [(Tomlinson et al., 2011-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c8bfb3872e88c45c1acc1528be4c1d2df59315d9), whose best conventional model fit for brass, using triangular ridge assumptions and a fixed loss fraction of 0.45, had a slope of 0.96 and R-squared of 0.78, and whose surface-design analysis found that coefficients of friction changed little until ridge width reached 0.28 mm and height reached 108 microns, then increased substantially for the largest 0.3 mm width and 250 micron height texture. This indicates that geometry-based friction coefficients can be predictive of friction force and texture effects, which is a potential baseline that instability-based predictions must exceed. The weakest contradiction is from [(Sergachev et al., 2021-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/322f92233f72b50913c369245230611ac0f61cfd), which tested silicone ellipsoidal asperity samples with 200 um lateral spacing and 30 or 50 um heights and found statistically significant asperity-height effects with paired t-test p-values of 1.2e-5 for E100 and 8.6e-6 for E150, and that a reference surface with Sa around 150 nm had lower friction than references with Sa between 40 and 60 nm. These results show that conventional friction and roughness metrics can detect texture differences, but the evidence is limited by one human subject, a single 2 mm/s sliding velocity, no perceptual accuracy measure, and no instability classification, so it is less directly contradictory to the proposed human discrimination method.",
    "suggested_action": "The most important action is to define the conventional baselines under exactly the same controlled conditions used for the instability metrics and to test them in the same GLMM framework: compute average friction coefficient from the stable sliding region, 3D roughness parameters such as Sa and Sq, contact angle hysteresis, and instability frequencies for each of the six silane coatings across the planned 5, 10, 25, and 45 mm/s velocities and 0, 25, 75, and 100 g plus 6 g finger weights, then compare model fit using AIC or BIC, likelihood-ratio tests, effect sizes, and confidence intervals for participant accuracy and response time. This directly addresses the proposed method's comparison goal and avoids the main weakness in the related work, where conventional metrics were measured under different protocols from perception or instability analysis. The second most important action is to validate contact angle hysteresis as a meaningful baseline because none of the related studies measured it; measure forward and receding contact angles on the same vapor-deposited silane coatings, report hysteresis with uncertainty, and include it as a covariate to ensure that any advantage of instability metrics is not simply due to unmeasured surface energy or wetting effects. Third, adopt the human-variability controls highlighted by [(Zhang et al., 2017-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c2abc15c408b0b8ef82c77555f23af75aa64115e): record or constrain sliding velocity, normal load, skin hydration, cleaning and air-drying time, stroke length, and trial order, and use random participant and surface-pair effects in the GLMM; given the plan's 10 participants and 600 trials, add a power analysis and consider subjective confidence or 0 to 10 roughness/stickiness/comfort ratings to triangulate forced-choice accuracy. Fourth, incorporate the mechanical-protocol lessons from [(Sergachev et al., 2021-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/322f92233f72b50913c369245230611ac0f61cfd), including preparatory strokes, stable-sliding extraction, removal of drive-mechanism noise, sequential loading when finger recovery is slow, and reporting load-dependent friction coefficients rather than a single average value; the planned 550 Hz sampling should be sufficient to capture stiction spikes and slow frictional waves, but the analysis should specify how steady sliding, slow waves, and stiction spikes are thresholded and validated. Finally, retain the geometry and material descriptors from [(Tomlinson et al., 2011-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c8bfb3872e88c45c1acc1528be4c1d2df59315d9) as secondary baselines, such as ridge or asperity width, height, and material-specific adhesion/interlocking contributions if the minimal surfaces have any detectable microstructure, but because the plan uses roughness below human detection limits, the primary conventional comparison should remain average friction coefficient, areal roughness, and contact angle hysteresis; also document computational resources, code, and reproducibility details for instability classification and GLMM fitting.",
